days so  I went in for a trial got the job and  super quickly after that it turned out  that that manager was super incompetent  and an alcoholic so he was fired  replaced by a manager from another store  who then laid off the entire staff  for me which just a terrible place to  sometimes a morale and they in clearly  picked her because the store was a mess  and needed work so she was like hot coal  and she was really strict about the  single worst thing about working there  which is kpi's basically when you go to  gamestop and they ask you if you have a  rewards card or they ask you if you want  to preorder anything or if you want any  accessories or if you want to buy the  game pre-owned instead of brand new  that's because the stats list it out and  has literally competitions internally in  the stores where they'll be like oh this  person got the most pre-owned is this  week good job why didn't you get as many  as them so if that asking you those  questions it's because there's so much  pressure from the top and I understand  kpi's but the way that was dealt with so  competitively was really gross in my  opinion to the extent that they would  encourage us to do some shady [ __ ] they  would encourage me to sell a pre-owned  up for a game that had just been  announced meaning it didn't even have a  release date that was once a legitimate  conversation that was along the lines of  if someone's buying a shooter you should  tell them that you've seen the latest  Call of Duty and you know that it's  awesome to encourage them to preorder it  even though you haven't I do look at two  of the store was like one that I would  fill in and one that I ended up being a  casual app that didn't tell me a lie  about [ __ ] but they was still just so  strict about kpi's in a way that just  never felt fair aside from that and I  mean I like not spent a lot of time  talking about this pod the walkouts was  just terrible in that you would have to  do tons of unpaid overtime even as  casual stuff you know I was like a 15  year old working in a store being told  to stay two hours late which is illegal  and thinking about it now the fact that  it happened to me when I was effectively  a child just seems so much more messed  up anytime there was a sale we would  have to make sure the stickers had been  changed on every single game before the  end of the 90  you said that one time my nail blood  from peeling stickers off of cases for  so long I started bleeding and I was  still bad doing it not getting paid for  it so yeah totally  shady working conditions but then there  were the customers which was next level  I don't even know where to start  there was this weird thing in the city  that I lived in don't know if this  happens in other cities or other stores  where people who are in some way  involved in selling drugs would you know  sell the drugs and trade them for an  Xbox and then they were using Xboxes and  Playstations kind of as currency in  these drug schemes so we would have the  same person come in multiple times  trading in a console or returning a  console and we would have to basically  take down notes for police reports that  we're looking for these people it was  hectic we would also sometimes have  people who would go up to games on the  shelf and swap the price sticker over  because I guess there's a law in  Australia where if something has the  wrong sticker on it on the wrong price  on it you have to sell it for the listed  price so we would watch people will take  off a sticker and swap it with another  sticker and then come to the count and  be like oh I would like to buy this game  and then you have to really quietly be  like oh I'm sorry um I just saw you swap  the stick is over I'm gonna have to give  it to you for the original price the  absolute was thing that happened a  million times was when a kid would come  into the store and try and buy an MA 15  plus game and you would have to tell  them no because they will underage you  have to check for ID and be like I can't  legally sell you this game because it is  rated higher than your age and then they  would leave and they would bring their  parents back with them and their parents  would just yell at you they would yell  at you for not selling the kid a game  that you legally couldn't sell them well  actually a child is 13 this is rated  full audiences over 15 years old because  it deals with mature themes are you  aware of it they just would just  lose their minds at you it was like you  were the biggest inconvenience in the  world because parents wouldn't do their  research on what kind of games that kid  was trying to buy it was just like  people had utter disregard for the fact  that part of your job was upholding the  law which is pretty unusual the same  thing happens with traded games you  can't accept trade-ins from anyone  unless that River 18 and I mean that was  a whole other story when you would tell  them that the price wasn't as high as  they expected like that's a meme or just  be so mad at you like you were the one  picking the goddamn prices like it  wasn't something that was set in stone  by the people at the head code is making  a hundred thousand dollars a year I did  also deal with like crappy behavior from  people being like oh you're a girl like  is there a man here I can speak to about  the video games oh like young guys guys  with teenagers just like completely  doubting my knowledge that stuff's  mostly laughable but also like sometimes  it's kind of fun when people would be  like oh is there a man around and I'd be  like I can grab one for you but try me  but hey all of that said then what parts  of it that I really enjoyed I liked a  lot of the people that I worked with  single best thing about working that was  that you know there's huge posters I  don't like wall size that they have  hanging at game stops I have a  collection of so many of those  Assassin's Creed - I have the lost of us  I think I have 